/* Generated by tools/export-vault-notes.py from the Obsidian vault. Re-run the script; do not edit by hand. */
Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A natural monopoly arises when the fixed costs are high in an industry, but the variable/marginal costs are low. This means that once production, which may be initially expensive, starts, the firm can quickly achieve an economy of scale.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Natural monopolies are often a key justification for the existence of some monopolies.</p><h4>Graphical Depiction</h4><p></p><h4>References</h4>","Before the Industrial Revolution, there was a disconnect between propositional knowledge and prescriptive knowledge":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While there were many technological innovations before the Industrial Revolution, there was also a disconnect between practical (prescriptive) knowledge and theoretical (propositional) knowledge.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This disconnect between the two forms of knowledge is the reason that the innovations were not able to produce economic growth.</p><h4>References</h4>","Capital's share of income will rise in the future due to AI":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In the future, due to the rise in the ability of capital to entirely replace human labor (rather than be complements), capital’s share of income will drastically increase.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the thesis of Thomas Piketty, and although he had been wrong about the past, this note argues that he will be correct in the future.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>While in the past, it has not been the case that capital has been able to largely replace labor (and thus occupy a larger share of national income), as Piketty predicted, mainly due to the fact that capital remained complementary to labor, in the future, especially due to AI, this is unlikely to be the case. AI is able to perform many agentic tasks that require complex cognitive functions, which has been the factor that has ensured that labor is complementary to capital (labor is needed to ensure that the repetitive work that capital does is directed in the right way). This means that they will likely begin to overtake labor’s share of income in the economy.</p><ul><li>This also means that wealth inequality could rise, since the people who originally had more wealth are better able to utilize AI (since they can hire “AI Advisors”), and thus generate wealth, and so on.</li><li>Additionally, while developing nations have traditionally relied on lower labor costs to attract work, now that capital is able to replace labor, these countries lose their comparative advantage there. Naturally resources are the only means for them to grow rich at that point.</li></ul><p>Some ways to potentially mitigate this is a strong wealth tax or inheritance tax.</p><h4>References</h4>","Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Culture has become the most important factor in determining the identity of people in a nation. Nations that are significantly divided between cultures is known as a “torn country.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that a nation’s stability can partly be determined by the cultural homogeneity present in its society.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Culture becoming the most important factor in the way that a society views itself means that</p><ul><li>There is the creation of out-groups, leading to feeling of superiority, fear and lack of trust in people of different cultures, differences in communication, and lack of familiarity.</li></ul><p>The cultural question is one that cannot be negotiated, since it is a zero-sum game. Additionally, generally, it seems that nations with more diversity of culture tends to be more unstable than nations with more homogeneity of culture.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West is in decline\">The West is in decline</a> <ul><li>Specifically, the culture of the West in decline in the areas of the world previously dominated by Western culture.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A ‘torn country’ can redefine itself\">A ‘torn country’ can redefine itself</a><ul><li>How a country that is cleavage by culture can reshape itself.</li></ul></li></ul>","Deontic powers, which are desire-independent reasons for acting, are built upon institutional facts":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Deontic power, or reasons for acting that goes beyond simple desire to do something, are created through institutional facts. This is because deontic power relates to the rights, duties, and obligations that come with institutional facts.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrates the consequences of having institutional facts.</p><h4>References</h4>","Elite overproduction could be a cause of social instability":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Elite overproduction could be the cause of social instability since when there is an excess supply of potential elite members in society, these elites are not able to achieve the status in society that they were promised, and thus, they will instigate social instability.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These could be a potential reason for social instability.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors\">Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors</a><ul><li>This is an example of when this self-correction goes wrong.</li></ul></li></ul>","Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The primary purpose of fair equality of opportunity is simply to ensure that no one is unjustly treated based on factors outside of their control (i.e., social contingencies).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This reveals the important of justice, since it is placed even above concerns of efficiency.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Rawls elaborates that even if restricting access to positions ultimately would produce better results, such actions would still not be justified. <code>What about for cases such as men wanting to model for female modeling positions? Or patients requesting female doctors but there are equally qualified male doctors? This principle seems awfully vague.</code></p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pure procedural justice is the most practical form of justice to evaluate the basic structure with\">Pure procedural justice is the most practical form of justice to evaluate the basic structure with</a><ul><li>Explains why pure procedural justice is used.</li></ul></li></ul>","In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is the little moments that shape us in life, not mainly the big, especially memorable ones.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Although the big events in life are what we remember, the big events are merely culminations of the little moments.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>What is most valuable in life is to live everyday with intention and vitality, since they are what ultimately shape us for the big moments in life. But the big moments is ultimately not the important part either. What actually matters for quality of life and satisfaction is the routines.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Right now, it seems to me that to live life well in this sense means to constantly see life through a new lens, to discover the undiscovered, and to approach life with a fortitude and endurance.</p><h4>References</h4>","Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Interlocking patents, when very minute pieces of information are able to be patented, stalls innovation rather than promoting it. This is because new inventions are built off of old ones, and if old technology is continually patented, it leaves no room for new innovation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a step in Chang’s argument against the strict patent and IPR laws that are in place.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>I think that there are some holes with this argument. Chang doesn’t specify what he means when he says that a piece of information is “minute.” Who is to say whether Amazon’s one click purchase is too small of an invention to patent? When does an idea become no longer minute?</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation\">Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs\">Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a></li></ul>","Large MES relative to industry demand indicates consolidation; small MES indicates competition":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When the minimum efficient scale (MES) is relatively large relative to the overall demand, there is likely to be high concentration. On the other hand, when it is relatively small, there is unlikely to be high concentration.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>By looking at the MES, we are able to predict roughly how concentrated an industry is, which may be useful.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p><strong>Small MES</strong> If the overall demand for bread in a city is 200,000 loaves in a month, and a bakery is able to produce 200 loaves in a month at its MES, then, since 200 is a relatively small proportion of 200,000, there is likely perfect or near perfect competition in the industry.</p><p><strong>Large MES</strong> If the overall demand for diamonds is 200 per month in a city, and a single jewelry store is able to produce 150 diamonds per month at its MES, since 150 is a relatively large proportion of 200, the market is likely to be highly concentrated.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry</a></li></ul>","Lying is a 'formal wrongdoing' and not a material wrong":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In the classic case of the axe murder, where an axe murder comes to your door and asks for the location of your friend with the intention of murdering them, Kant says that you are obligated to tell the truth. By lying, however, the harm done is a formal one, not a material one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This clears up a common point of misunderstanding regarding Kant’s legal and ethical views.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A common interpretation of Kant’s condemnation of lying to the murderer is to interpret Kant’s desire not to lie to the murderer as one that is characterized by the murderer having a “right to truth.” Therefore, in lying, you are doing a “material wrong” to the murderer. However, this doesn’t actually seem to be the case. Rather, you are actually simply doing a “formal wrong” in the sense that you are wronging society as a whole by eroding, if only ever so slightly, the structures of trust that underlie a society. This erodes the argument that Kant’s original claim was “morally repugnant,” since it is no longer any potential harm to the murderer that we are referring to. Rather, it is a more general damage to society that is the wrong which the lying produces.</p><h4>References</h4>","Mohists were mainly derived from the lower classes of society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The core precepts of Mohism, that of universal love, was something that was greatly appeal to the lower classes, who had something to gain from a moral levelling out with the aristocracy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes the major support group and believers of Mohism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are a few main critiques of Confucianism leveled by Mohism, and these critiques are motivated by the social position of the Mohsists:</p><ol><li>Confucianism does not believe in the existence of the gods or spirits.</li><li>Confucianism is too ritualistic (despite the fact that they are not believers in the gods or spirits).</li><li>Confucianism advocated for predestination (命), which caused people to be lazy and resign themselves to their fate.</li></ol><p>The third criticism is obviously misdirected, since although Confucianists knew the circumstances are allotted, one’s moral achievements are not. One tries their best to be moral, though <em>ming</em> may, in the end, disrupt their progress.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>A defense of the argument presented in 2 says that Confucian rituals were mostly to pay respects to the ancestors. Additionally, its seems that the reason for Mohism’s assertion of the existence of gods and spirits is merely in order to provide <em>ethos</em> for the arguments of <em>Mozi</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>Education was something that was only accessible to people with means.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives\">Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives</a><ul><li>These categorical imperatives would enforce the existing social hierarchy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Moral responsibility divides into answerability, attributability, and accountability":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>To hold someone responsible may mean demanding their reasons (answerability), judging the act as expressing who they are (attributability), or holding them liable to praise and blame (accountability), and these three come apart.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Arguments about responsibility often equivocate between the three, so distinguishing them dissolves a class of apparent disagreements.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Accountability is the demanding sense, since it licenses the reactive attitudes of resentment, indignation and guilt. Attributability is the weakest, since an act can be attributable to a person’s character without their being liable to blame for it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An agent remains responsible for determined actions that express their character\">An agent remains responsible for determined actions that express their character</a><ul><li>A view that secures attributability while leaving accountability open.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible\">One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible</a><ul><li>Bears on which of the three senses survives a determinist picture.</li></ul></li></ul>","New technologies can upset the existing power balance by lowering the cost of doing so":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When new technologies arise, they tend to lower the marginal cost of disrupting the extant power hierarchy. Thus the means of disruption are democratized, and thus leads to more violence/chaos/change of the status quo.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a historical pattern that has shown up in the past and seems to be able to applied to analyze contemporary situations as well.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The development of iron smithing during the end of the Bronze Age is theorized as a big reason for the collapse of the Bronze Age empires and the subsequent creation of localized kingdoms rather than centralized empires.</p><ul><li>Iron meant that it was easier to equip large armies that could challenge the state, and the state is especially able to overwhelmed when challenges come from varies regions simultaneously.</li><li>Because a monopoly on bronze was what had allowed empires to maintain their centralization previously, with the democratization of the means of violence, such a monopoly disappears, and thus, no highly centralized state was able to appear for some time.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should never halt technological innovation\">We should never halt technological innovation</a><ul><li>It seems that the people who are currently in charge might have some incentive to do so though.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement\">Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement</a><ul><li>It is possible that this is complementary factor to the effect described in this note in promoting instability.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"State dissolution often begins with devolution of power\">State dissolution often begins with devolution of power</a><ul><li>“Devolution of power” could also be interpreted as “devolution of the means to power,” i.e., the distribution of new, disruptive technologies.</li></ul></li></ul>","Non-compete clauses are bad for the worker and the consumer":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is evidence to suggest that there are adverse effects to having non-compete clauses, since they reduce wages for laborers and raise prices for the consumer.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a potential argument for bans of non-compete clauses.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>When non-compete clauses disappear, workers wages rise by 3-4% across a variety of fields.</li><li>Although NCC encourages training, the worker does not share in the gain, as the ROI of the training is captured entirely by the firm.</li><li>It hurts small businesses since they are not able to hire many people due to non-compete contracts.</li><li>It also hurts consumers since NCCs lead to reduced labor mobility, high market concentration, and thus higher prices.</li><li>Bans of NCCs lead to knowledge spillovers.</li><li>They are also not any more effective in preventing information leakage than NDAs.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The labor market is not perfectly competitive\">The labor market is not perfectly competitive</a><ul><li>The underlying condition. Non-competes can only suppress wages because labour markets have monopsony power to begin with.</li></ul></li></ul>","Out-migration taxes are a method of preventing tax flight":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While just implementing a higher wealth tax may lead to substantial tax flight, by implementing out-migration taxes, you are able to regain much of the lost tax revenue, since people are much less incentivized to leave the country when there is a out-migration tax.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a strategy that states can use to ensure that they are able to maximize tax revenue even when they raise taxes on the rich.</p><h4>Graphs and Charts</h4><p> As shown in the data, when a wealth tax is combined with an out-migration tax, the net effect is a positive one, since tax revenues rise in general, and so does aggregate output.  This shows the effectiveness of out-migration taxes, since it pushes down the out-migration rate of the most productive business owners down to levels equivalent to the unproductive business owners.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes\">An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes</a><ul><li>Another tax proposal that is related to the idea of taxing the rich. Perhaps that proposal likewise has some solution that mitigates the negative consequences?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The profit motive sharpens incentives\">The profit motive sharpens incentives</a><ul><li>Something that is clearly demonstrate here, as when profits lower (via the out-migration tax), business owners are incentivized to remain in the country.</li></ul></li></ul>","People sin so that they may be loved":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>People can motivate their sinful actions by the fact that they want to be loved.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is apparent through the actions of many people throughout different novels.</p><h4><em>East Of Eden</em></h4><p>Charles Trask offers this example when he beats up Adam because Cyrus loves Adam more than Charles. Caleb Track offers an example of this in the next generation, since he engages in the bean business, making a profit at the cost of the nation (price gouging), in order to please Adam.</p><ul><li>After this, he takes Aron to see Kate, which shatters Aron’s worldview.</li></ul><h4><em>All The King’s Men</em></h4><p>Stark engages in ruthless political tactics in order to get what he wants, with is praise from the people (but at the same time, it seems that he genuinely also wants to help the people).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism fails\">Utilitarianism fails</a><ul><li>It seems that if utilitarianism view, this view of this note cannot be supported, and is a behavior that should be discouraged.</li></ul></li></ul>","Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The originally monetarist thesis (now largely accepted in the mainstream) that the amount of money in an economy is the driver of inflation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core concept that underlies Monetarist thought.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The effect can be described by the following equation: $$ MV=PY $$ or, equivalently: $$ \\Delta \\% M + \\Delta \\% V = \\Delta \\% P + \\Delta \\% Y $$ , where $M$ is the money supply, $V$ is the velocity of money, $P$ is the price level in the economy, and $Y$ is the real GDP. Given this, and holding $Y$ and $V$ constant, we can see that a change in $M$ corresponds to a commensurate change in $P$.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus\">Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus</a><ul><li>An alternative theory to explain inflation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect\">A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect</a><ul><li>The effect of a change in price level.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Catholic Church allows for the establishment of universal Christian morality above secular spheres of political action":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The main benefit of the Catholic church lies in the fact that it is able to ensure a separation of spiritual and political power such that the church is able to use its universal spiritual power to order the political world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Comte’s view of the Catholic church.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the Catholic church is supremely and universally involved in spiritual matters, its monopoly on this power allows for it to be able to mediate earthly affairs in accordance with its moral principles. In this way, it is both able to maintain order and also allow for progression in society by aligning the world more closely with Christian morality.</p><ul><li>By Comte’s time, however, the spiritual power of the church had diminished greatly, and no longer was able to act in the way specified here.</li><li>Specifically, also, what Comte is interested in is the <em>organization</em> of the church, not necessarily the beliefs of the church.</li></ul><p>In Comte’s philosophy, the role that the church serves is one of the organization of the three “levels” of human thought: theological, metaphysical, and positive. The organization of the Catholic church is preserved, but as a vessel for positivist ideas.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Protestantism represents a deviation this ideal. While there is “progress” in the sense that people are able to engage in free inquiry, such a system lacks social order, since social order is incompatible with the continual discussion of the very foundations of society by people who are not competent enough to be able to engage in such discussions.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The final stage of history is a positive one\">The final stage of history is a positive one</a><ul><li>It is precisely the Catholic church that enables this final stage to be a positive one.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Frankfurt School shaped postmodernism and modern progressive activism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that modern progressive activism was shaped by the Frankfurt School, though many of the core leftism tenants are perversions of the original thought of the Frankfurt School.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the ideological origins of the modern progressive activism movement.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The Frankfurt School practiced <em>negative dialectics</em>, which involved describing a concept by what it is not.</li><li>The Frankfurt School opposed political activism, since they opposed propaganda and the adulteration of their philosophy with propaganda.</li><li>They also invented the method of examining society not just through the economic substructure, but also, directly from the cultural superstructure.</li></ul><p>The modern progressivism has adopted the second of these and has weakened the central philosophical core of the first move through critique as merely critique, and nothing more. Herbert Marcuse was the person who took Frankfurt School philosophy out of the abstract and began advocating for political activism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The bias of researchers manifest clearly through their research (typically left)\">The bias of researchers manifest clearly through their research (typically left)</a><ul><li>This is part of the influence of the Frankfurt School.</li></ul></li></ul>","The introduction of bureaucracy into the housing construction with section 701 led to an increase in single family homes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that introducing bureaucracy, especially bureaucracy that places the ability to regulate housing construction on the local level promotes the construction of single family homes (for example, section 701 of the Urban Planning Assistance Program), since that is in the interests of the existing homeowners.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the ineffectiveness of past regulations that initially were established to ensure that housing would be more plentiful.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Section 701 of the Urban Planning Assistance Program introduced bureaucracy into the process of new housing construction. This meant a shift towards the new construction of less dense, single family homes, rather than higher density apartments. In this way, the government introduced the planning capacity to the local cities that allowed them to take charge of development, meaning that the existing homeowners were able to capitalize on their preferences and ensure that future construction would also be single family homes.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The introduction of bureaucracy into the housing construction with section 701 led to an increase in single family homes\">The introduction of bureaucracy into the housing construction with section 701 led to an increase in single family homes</a><ul><li>Policies like the one mentioned in this note could be the reason that people don’t believe in “supply-side solutions,” since this one failed.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>This is a good example.</li></ul></li></ul>","The principle of insufficient reason is false":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The principle of insufficient reason is false since when you have no information, you simply cannot make any judgements about the nature of the probability distribution. Probabilities must be based in particular believes.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Rawls uses this as a part of his argument to reject average utilitarianism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism\">Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism</a><ul><li>Still, Rawls rejects average utilitarianism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>This is part of the reason why.</li></ul></li></ul>","There are many possible ways to hurt someone in a transaction":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are many ways of hurting someone through a transaction, some methods being just, and others not.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is how Aristotle’s ethics would decide whether someone is unjust in some way.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The two types of harm-causing acts are not unjust.</p><ul><li><strong>Misadventure</strong>: causing a harm though the harm occurring was “contrary to reasonable expectations.”</li><li><strong>Mistake</strong>: causing a harm that is not “contrary to reasonable expectations,” though there was no viciousness intended in the harm.</li></ul><p>These two types of harm-causing acts can be unjust.</p><ul><li><strong>Injury</strong>: causing a harm with prior knowledge and expectation, but no deliberation. </li><li><strong>Harming from choice</strong>: causing harm with prior knowledge, deliberation, and expectation of doing harm.</li></ul><p>In short, for an act to not constitute being unjust, the act has to have to been done out of <em>ignorance</em>, rather than “unnatural and subhuman reactions” or deliberate intention.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rectificatory justice concerns undoing a transaction between individuals\">Rectificatory justice concerns undoing a transaction between individuals</a><ul><li>This is where the contents of this note are useful.</li></ul></li></ul>","There is a much higher concentration of potassium inside a cell compared to sodium":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to $Na+/P^+-ATPase$, there is a much higher concentration of potassium inside a cell compared to outside of it, and a much lower concentration of sodium compared to outside.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>It is this process which generate membrane potential and a concentration gradient in a cell.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A sodium-potassium pump is the most important protein generating membrane potential in animal cells\">A sodium-potassium pump is the most important protein generating membrane potential in animal cells</a></li></ul>","There is an inherent ontological hierarchy in nature":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is an natural order to things in the world, in that “lower” things are meant to serve “higher” things.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a fundamental pillar of Aristotle’s metaphysical world view. Also, this metaphysical view continued to be held into the middle ages in the form of the Great Chain of Being.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This concept is what ultimately justifies things like:</p><ul><li>Natural slavery,</li><li>The subordination of women,</li><li>The merely instrumental value of animals and plants,</li><li>The ranking of lives and activites, e.g. The contemplative life outweighs the political.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a><ul><li>There is an ordering, for example, of justice where political justice is at the top.</li></ul></li></ul>","We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The mainstream accepted theory of humor, which states that we find something funny because what actually occurs represents an incongruence with our perceptions of what ought to occur, or simply what we thought would occur.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents the most widely accepted theory of humor today.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A comedian tells a joke that includes a pun that changes the entire meaning of the sentence to something that is dark or otherwise unexpected.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because it grants us relief\">We find something funny because it grants us relief</a><ul><li>A different theory.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority\">We find something funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority</a><ul><li>Another theory.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
